    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-129892",
      "knowledge_point_id": "d5baf28d-c2c0-57bd-9433-c47043009f0d",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Curve",
      "problem": "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x+y}{x-y}$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(-2,-1).$",
      "worked_solution": "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(x,y)=(-2,-1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-2,-1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(-2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x+y}{x-y})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x+y}{x-y})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1(x-y)-(x+y)(1)}{(x-y)^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{1(x-y)-(x+y)(-1)}{(x-y)^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{2y}{(x-y)^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2x}{(x-y)^{2}}\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-2,-1) & =-\\frac{2(-1)}{(-2-(-1))^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2(-2)}{(-2-(-1))^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i-4\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(-2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(-2,-1)}{‖∇f(-2,-1)‖} & =\\frac{2\\,i-4\\,j}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-4)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{5}}(2\\,i-4\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}(i-2\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}(\\,i-2\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
