    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-71383",
      "knowledge_point_id": "d5baf28d-c2c0-57bd-9433-c47043009f0d",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Curve",
      "problem": "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(5,4).$",
      "worked_solution": "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(5,4)$, can be found by computing $∇f(5,4).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(5,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}})\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\text{ }i-\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}}\\text{ }j \\\\ ∇f(5,4) & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{(5)^{2}-(4)^{2}}}\\text{ }i-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{(5)^{2}-(4)^{2}}}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{9}}i-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{9}}j \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}i-\\frac{4}{3}j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇f(5,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇f(5,4)}{‖∇f(5,4)‖} & =\\frac{\\frac{5}{3}i-\\frac{4}{3}j}{\\sqrt{{(\\frac{5}{3})}^{2}+{(-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{5}{3}i-\\frac{4}{3}j}{\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{41}}{41}(5i-4j)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
