      "math_academy_id": "q-36346",
      "knowledge_point_id": "d5baf28d-c2c0-57bd-9433-c47043009f0d",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Curve",
      "problem": "Find a unit normal vector to the level curve of the function $g(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy)$ that passes through the point where $(x,y)=(1,1).$",
      "worked_solution": "A normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point  where $(x,y)=(1,1)$, can be found by computing $∇g(1,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient of $g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at $(x,y)=(1,1):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇g(x,y) & ={g}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{g}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xy))\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{xy}\\cdot y\\,i+\\frac{1}{xy}\\cdot x\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,i+\\frac{1}{y}\\,j \\\\ ∇g(1,1) & =\\frac{1}{(1)}\\,i+\\frac{1}{(1)}\\,j \\\\  & =i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we normalize $∇g(1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∇g(1,1)}{‖∇g(1,1)‖} & =\\frac{i+j}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
