      "math_academy_id": "q-71404",
      "knowledge_point_id": "4516cb28-0fc0-5c15-b9b2-747c3827e189",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding a Normal Vector to a Level Surface",
      "problem": "For the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2}-xy)-yz$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0).$",
      "worked_solution": "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(1,-2,0).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2}-xy)-yz)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2}-xy)-yz)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({z}^{2}-xy)-yz)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{y}{{z}^{2}-xy}\\text{ }i+(-\\frac{x}{{z}^{2}-xy}-z)\\text{ }j+(\\frac{2z}{{z}^{2}-xy}-y)\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(1,-2,0) & =-\\frac{(-2)}{(0)^{2}-(1)(-2)}\\,i+(-\\frac{1}{(0)^{2}-(1)(-2)}-0)\\,j+(\\frac{2(0)}{(0)^{2}-(1)(-2)}-(-2))\\,k \\\\  & =i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $i-\\frac{1}{2}\\,j+2\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-2,0).$",
